Haakon Studebaker schrieb:
> An amulet of reincarnation. It's a modified amulet of life saving. When
> you are killed, you are brought back [...] Your
> level will remain the same and your alignment will remain the same since
> the deity which reincarnated you does not wish to lose a follower.

Hmm, a sort of low-grade amulet of life saving? Nice idea, but i don't
think re-incarnation fits the ticket.

As i understand it, reincarnation isn't a work of the deity you
follow, but rather a metaphysical automatism (or in fact even the
doing of the evil demiurge who shaped the world and its laws so as to
keep souls in the prison of material existence). 

There might be some eastern myth of privileged people changing into a
whole new adult body[1], but in the western re-incarnation schools,
having to go through the troubles of being born is part of the severe
punishment physical existence and re-incarnation represent.

[1] i suspect that this might be reserved to persons of practically
divine status, though; i've just finished reading something on the
Cathars (a christian sect which believed in reincarnation) and _they_
believed that Jesus was the only human-shaped being who escaped the
humiliation of being born by so vile a thing ("in re tam vili") as a
woman (the material world was entirely evil to them, and the female
body was the tool by which children came into this world - the
ultimate tool of evil). Cathar doctrine is not too well-known, though;
a lot has to be deduced from interrogations of lay people, and lay
cathars had no better knowledge of doctrine than lay catholics.

> If your deity is angry with you, have bad luck or both then you may be
> reincarnated at a lower experience level or you may not be reincarnated
> at all.

Going by the common reincarnation beliefs, not being reincarnated is
the ultimate *reward* everyone should be striving for. I guess you
might _not_ be re-incarnated if you died a "good" death while being
the crowned champion of your alignment (you'd go straight to Nirvana
or the lost Paradise); in every other case, you _would_ re-incarnate,
but you'd lose all your memories.

